    {
      "id": "al13-l2",
      "title": "The Stack",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Where does CALL remember the address? On the stack: a piece of memory at the end, used from the top down. The register SP (stack pointer) points to its top.",
        "CALL pushes the return address (2 bytes) onto the stack, RET pops it off. Calls inside calls just stack up, which is why it's called a stack.",
        "LXI SP (061) sets the stack pointer. On a 256-byte Altair, SP starts at the very end: address 400 (just past 377), written as 000 001."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "061   ; 000: LXI SP",
          "000   ;      address 400 (low byte)",
          "001   ;      (high byte)",
          "076   ; 003: MVI A",
          "001   ;      1",
          "315   ; 005: CALL a",
          "013   ;      address 013 (low byte)",
          "000   ;      (high byte)",
          "323   ; 010: OUT 377",
          "377   ;      port 377",
          "166   ; 012: HLT",
          "207   ; 013: ADD A   ← a",
          "315   ; 014: CALL b",
          "020   ;      address 020 (low byte)",
          "000   ;      (high byte)",
          "311   ; 017: RET",
          "306   ; 020: ADI   ← b",
          "012   ;      10",
          "311   ; 022: RET"
        ],
        "output": [
          "LEDS ○○○○●●○○ = 12"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "al13-l2-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Where does CALL store the return address?",
          "options": [
            "On the stack",
            "In register B",
            "At address 000",
            "In the program"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "SP points to the top of the stack."
        },
        {
          "id": "al13-l2-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Which way does the stack grow?",
          "options": [
            "Down, towards smaller addresses",
            "Up",
            "It doesn't move",
            "Randomly"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "It starts at the end of memory."
        },
        {
          "id": "al13-l2-e3",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "How many bytes does one return address take on the stack?",
          "options": [
            "2",
            "1",
            "3",
            "8"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "An address has two bytes."
        },
        {
          "id": "al13-l2-e4",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What do the lights show?",
          "code": [
            "061   ; 000: LXI SP",
            "000   ;      address 400 (low byte)",
            "001   ;      (high byte)",
            "076   ; 003: MVI A",
            "001   ;      1",
            "315   ; 005: CALL a",
            "013   ;      address 013 (low byte)",
            "000   ;      (high byte)",
            "323   ; 010: OUT 377",
            "377   ;      port 377",
            "166   ; 012: HLT",
            "207   ; 013: ADD A   ← a",
            "315   ; 014: CALL b",
            "020   ;      address 020 (low byte)",
            "000   ;      (high byte)",
            "311   ; 017: RET",
            "306   ; 020: ADI   ← b",
            "012   ;      10",
            "311   ; 022: RET"
          ],
          "options": [
            "12",
            "11",
            "2",
            "1"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "a doubles 1 to 2, then calls b, which adds 10."
        },
        {
          "id": "al13-l2-e5",
          "type": "fill_blank",
          "prompt": "Set the stack pointer.",
          "code": [
            "___   ; LXI SP",
            "000   ; low byte",
            "001   ; high byte: address 400"
          ],
          "options": [
            "061",
            "041",
            "001",
            "311"
          ],
          "answer": "061",
          "explanation": "LXI SP = 0 6 1."
        },
        {
          "id": "al13-l2-e6",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What happens if a program uses too much stack on a 256-byte Altair?",
          "options": [
            "The stack runs into the program and overwrites it",
            "Nothing",
            "It gets more memory",
            "The Altair beeps"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Memory is tiny: stack and program share it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "al13-l3",
      "title": "Reusing a Routine",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "A good subroutine does one job, takes its input in a register and gives its result in a register. Then any program can use it.",
        "A delay loop, a multiply routine, a print routine: Altair owners swapped such routines in club newsletters, printed as octal listings.",
        "Write down which registers a routine uses: a CALL can change them without the caller noticing."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "076   ; 000: MVI A",
          "006   ;      6",
          "016   ; 002: MVI C",
          "007   ;      7",
          "315   ; 004: CALL mul",
          "012   ;      address 012 (low byte)",
          "000   ;      (high byte)",
          "323   ; 007: OUT 377",
          "377   ;      port 377",
          "166   ; 011: HLT",
          "107   ; 012: MOV B,A   ← mul",
          "257   ; 013: XRA A",
          "201   ; 014: ADD C   ← loop",
          "005   ; 015: DCR B",
          "302   ; 016: JNZ loop",
          "014   ;      address 014 (low byte)",
          "000   ;      (high byte)",
          "311   ; 021: RET"
        ],
        "output": [
          "LEDS ○○●○●○●○ = 42"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "al13-l3-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What makes a subroutine easy to reuse?",
          "options": [
            "One job, input and output in registers",
            "It's long",
            "It uses JMP",
            "It ends with HLT"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "Then you can CALL it from anywhere."
        },
        {
          "id": "al13-l3-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "In the example, which registers does mul change?",
          "options": [
            "A and B",
            "Only A",
            "C",
            "None"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "B counts, A collects the product."
        },
        {
